        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What equipment do I need for boxing at home?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At minimum: hand wraps, a pair of boxing gloves and something to hit, usually a free-standing or hanging bag. A jump rope is cheap and useful for conditioning and footwork. A double-end bag, a round timer and a rubber floor mat are good additions. Headgear, mouthguards and sparring gloves are for supervised partner work, not for training alone at home.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best punching bag for home use?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on where it will go. In a flat or a rented home, a free-standing bag such as the Century Original Wavemaster avoids mounting altogether. In a garage or basement with a sound structural joist, beam or a proper stand, a hanging heavy bag such as the Hayabusa Heavy Bag gives a more traditional feel. Check the floor or structure first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a free-standing or hanging punching bag better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A hanging bag swings and absorbs hard punches in a way most boxers prefer, but it needs a properly rated mount or stand. A free-standing bag needs no drilling, moves out of the way and suits renters, but it rocks under hard hits and its filled base is very heavy. For most homes the deciding factor is mounting, not boxing style.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use a punching bag in an apartment?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often, with care. A free-standing bag on a thick rubber mat is the usual choice, because a ceiling mount passes vibration into the building and may not be allowed. Expect some thud to reach the floor below. Train at sensible hours, favour lighter and faster combinations, and ask someone downstairs whether they can hear it. Check your lease or building rules first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What size boxing gloves should I get for a heavy bag?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Many adults use 12 to 16-ounce gloves on a heavy bag. Hayabusa, for example, suggests 10 to 12 ounces for bags and pads and 14 to 18 ounces for bags, partner drills and sparring. Heavier hitters often prefer 14 or 16 ounces for more padding. If you will spar at a gym, ask what weight they require; many ask for 16 ounces.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need hand wraps to hit a punching bag?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Wraps support the wrist and hold the small bones and joints of the hand together inside the glove, and hitting a heavy bag without them is a common way to hurt a hand or wrist. A 180-inch wrap suits most adult hands. Wrap firmly but not so tightly that your fingers tingle, and wash wraps regularly.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

